WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.770
Speaker 1: If you thought Mike Pence's campaign
announcement in Iowa yesterday was bad, just

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:10.719
wait until you get a look at his CNN town
hall, which was hosted by Dana Bash.

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:15.559
You know, one of the things that is very interesting
to see with these modern right wingers is

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:22.350
how quickly their talking points fall apart,
not when presented with opposition per se

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.940
from the other side, but simply when there
is any follow up.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.840
And Dana Bash, as you will see during some
of these clips, provides just really simple

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.930
questions that Mike Pence at some point doesn't
even know how to respond to now.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:45.309
To his credit, I will say Pence is starting
to draw some distinction with Donald Trump.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:49.260
And you've got to do that if you're going
to justify people voting for you instead of

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.670
Trump when you served under Trump, why would
they go for the number two if they liked the

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.359
number one when number one is running?

00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:57.079
Okay, you understand that?

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:03.059
Here is Pence saying Trump thinks Putin's
a genius.

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:05.390
I think Putin is a war criminal.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.390
All right.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.390
At least it's a distinction.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:13.260
Speaker 2: Donald Trump said in his CNN town
hall he was asked whether he wanted Ukraine

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.180
to win the war and he would not say.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.670
What message does that send to Vladimir Putin?

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:26.329
Speaker 3: Well, and frankly, when Vladimir
Putin rolled into Ukraine, the former president

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:29.000
called him a genius.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.530
Oh, I know the difference between a genius
and a war criminal.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.530
Right.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.759
And I know who needs to win in the war in
Ukraine.

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:42.290
And it's the people fighting for their freedom
and fighting to restore their national sovereignty.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:43.869
And Ukraine and America.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:45.729
It's not our war.

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:50.050
But freedom is our fight and we need to give
the people of Ukraine the ability to fight.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:54.590
Speaker 1: And the time for even the applause
is milquetoast.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.180
Like, it's not just the answers.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.579
Nobody seems in any way inspired by this.

00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:01.649
But listen to Pence's credit.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:06.580
There are other people running in this Republican
primary who aren't even willing to say that.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.420
So credit where credit is due.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.220
Even if it's for pathetically small things.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.890
Now, here was a really interesting moment.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:23.569
Dana Bash seemed to visibly stumped Mike Pence
when she pointed out, you know, deterrence,

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:30.530
claiming that the death penalty for shootings
will be a deterrent doesn't make sense when

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.440
many of the mass shooters want to be killed.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.310
And Pence was visibly stumped by this.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:37.310
A very interesting.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.310
Speaker 2: Moment.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:43.390
Quickly, go back to something you said about
expediting the processing of people who engage

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.510
in mass shootings.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.200
A lot of people who are mass shooters, they
go in with the intention to die.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.200
Right.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.819
So how would the threat of execution be a
deterrent?

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:55.760
Speaker 3: Well.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.670
I follow these stories closely as you do.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:05.549
And of course, our years in the White House,
we saw one tragedy after another.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:12.940
And I know we hear that and we we see evidence
oftentimes in the aftermath that they went

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.190
in without regard to whether they would survive.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:18.190
Right.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:25.440
But I just believe in the deterrent of the
law are and I believe perhaps if if we made

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.690
it clear I mean, think about this.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.360
The Parkland shooter is going to spend the
rest of his life in jail.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.790
Drew in Florida.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.640
And that's not justice.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.220
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:43.170
He believes in the deterrent of law.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:48.970
But that passing stricter gun laws wouldn't
make a difference.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:50.530
How is that possible?

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:55.470
The death penalty would be a deterrent, even
though, as Dana Bash correctly points out,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.610
many of these shooters plan to die.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:58.980
They want to die.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.810
Sometimes they take their own lives if they
aren't taken by by first responders.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:06.540
So that's going to be a deterrent.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.340
But stricter gun laws won't be a deterrent.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.330
That doesn't really make any sense.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:16.600
It's as incoherent as many of these modern
Republican positions.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:24.320
Mike Pence talked falsely about how Democrats
want abortion on demand until the moment of

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.170
birth, which of course, isn't true.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:28.790
And Dana Bash points that out.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:33.250
Speaker 3: At the Democrat Party's position
is abortion on demand with taxpayer dollars

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:34.500
all the way up to the moment of.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.500
Speaker 1: Birth?

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.500
Says who?

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.350
Speaker 3: And as we talk about the possibility
of a national standard, say, a 15 week standard,

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:46.160
which would put America more in line with
most of the European Union, most of the European

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.000
countries have either a 12 to 15 week rule
on abortion.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.310
Our laws today are more in line with China,
Iran and North Korea.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.650
And for my part, if I have the great privilege
to serve as president of the United States,

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:05.650
I'll all support the cause of life at every
level, the state level, the local level with

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.139
taxpayer dollars and at the national level.

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:11.750
Speaker 2: And just the person you're running
against or you want to run against if you

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.190
get the nomination is President Biden.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:18.560
He says that codifying Roe versus Wade is
his position.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.350
That means a right to an abortion until the
point of viability, not up until until birth.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:31.120
Speaker 1: And Mike Pence smirking at being
told the truth about the widespread opposition

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.440
from Joe Biden on that issue.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:40.030
On the question of Trump's crimes, Pence seems
to be going with yes, Trump committed crimes

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:44.669
and attempted to violate the Constitution,
but we kind of should just move on with no

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:45.669
consequences.

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:48.169
Speaker 3: I think now more than ever, we
ought to be finding ways we could actually

00:05:48.169 --> 00:05:49.169
come together.

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:52.280
We're going to get this kind of this kind
of action by the Department of Justice, I

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.060
think would only fuel further division in
the country.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:01.539
And let me also say, I think it would also
send a terrible message to the wider world.

00:06:01.539 --> 00:06:05.600
I mean, we are the we're the emblem of democracy.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.800
We're the symbol of justice our way.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:13.220
And the really the serious matter, which has
already happened once in New York.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:17.250
Of indicting a former president of the United
States sends a terrible message to the world.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:21.732
I hope the DOJ thinks better of it or resolves
these issues without an indictment.

00:06:21.732 --> 00:06:25.750
Speaker 2: I just want to clarify what you're
saying is that if they believe he committed

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.770
a crime, they should not go forward with an
indictment.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:31.420
You just talked before about.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:32.420
No.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:35.259
Speaker 1: By the way, anybody else noticing
how Pence does this weird thing where while

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:37.720
Dana Bash is talking to him, he doesn't look
at her.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:39.319
He just looks out into the middle distance.

00:06:39.319 --> 00:06:40.750
It's a very strange thing.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:41.979
And Pence does this to a lot of people.

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:43.110
Speaker 2: I think, to the rule of law.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.100
Speaker 3: Let me be clear that no one's above
the law.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.509
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:51.110
Speaker 3: But with regard to the unique circumstances
here.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:54.889
Speaker 1: No one's above the law.

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:55.889
But but, but.

00:06:55.889 --> 00:06:58.630
Okay, just a couple more of these.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:05.289
Pence says that he has no interest in pardoning
the individuals convicted for rioting on January

00:07:05.289 --> 00:07:06.289
6th.

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:07.330
Speaker 3: The Constitution says.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:08.510
Speaker 2: Sure on that.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:14.060
Donald Trump says he is, quote, inclined to
pardon many members of the mob who attacked

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:15.509
the Capitol on January six.

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:21.260
Those people were, of course, part of the
same mob who built gallows and chanted about

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:22.789
wanting to hang you.

00:07:22.789 --> 00:07:23.789
Right.

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:25.800
Would you consider parting and pardoning any
of them?

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:33.400
Speaker 3: You know, on the day of January
six, I issued a tweet demanding that people

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:34.650
leave the folks.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:35.650
Speaker 1: He tweeted.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:36.650
Speaker 3: And end the violence.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.310
And I said that those that failed to do that
should be prosecuted to the fullest extent

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:41.310
of the law.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:42.310
Right.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:49.169
And I continue to believe that today we cannot
ever allow what happened on January six to

00:07:49.169 --> 00:07:51.820
happen again in the heart of our democracy.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:57.780
What about the stand by the decisions and
the due process of court in our laws?

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:05.400
And I have no interest or no intention of
pardoning those that assaulted police officers

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.729
or vandalized our Capitol.

00:08:06.729 --> 00:08:07.729
Speaker 1: All right.

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:12.040
Now understand that even there he's saying
he has no interest in pardoning those who

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:16.400
assaulted police officers or vandalized the
Capitol.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.050
But if there are people who were charged for
other things, he doesn't seem to be ruling

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:26.229
that out, although with Pence, you never really
know what he means with his slippery language.

00:08:26.229 --> 00:08:31.710
And then lastly, Pence refusing to rule out
pardoning Donald Trump if he were to be elected

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:32.729
president.

00:08:32.729 --> 00:08:34.979
Sir, if Donald Trump.

00:08:34.979 --> 00:08:38.410
Speaker 2: If Donald Trump is convicted of
a crime.

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:39.940
And you're elected president.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:40.940
Would you pardon him?

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:44.640
Speaker 3: Well, I don't want to speak about
hypotheticals.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.200
Oh.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.230
I'm not sure I'm going to be elected president
of the United States.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.630
Speaker 1: I'm sure that you're not.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:59.040
But okay, Dana, I'm going to decline to answer
that on the basis that I have absolutely no

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.690
path to the nomination whatsoever.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:05.080
And I don't even know why I'm really here.

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:08.149
So on that basis, I'm going to decline to
answer that question.

00:09:08.149 --> 00:09:10.829
Speaker 3: But I believe we have a fighting
chance.

00:09:10.829 --> 00:09:12.010
I really believe we do.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.060
Speaker 2: And if you are.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:21.110
Speaker 3: And I would hope again, Dana, in
your ear, as persistent as ever.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.550
CNN is living up to its reputation.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.680
And look, there are real issues the American
people are facing.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.080
And rather than talking about that, I want
to talk about.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:30.680
Speaker 1: So he doesn't want to answer that
question.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:37.320
So a disastrous campaign launch for Mike Pence,
no path to the nomination.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:42.760
But could he end up helping Trump in Trump's
path to the Republican nomination?

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.769
We'll talk about that maybe tomorrow, maybe
on Monday

